# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# App variables
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Variables of this application only, appended to infra/compose/.env.example
# after the platform's by `platform-infra sync` (this file is APP-OWNED: it is
# created once and never overwritten; .env.example itself is generated). Add a
# variable here as KEY=value with its explanation on the lines above it, then
# run the sync. Never add a commented-out KEY=value line: the deploy wizard
# reads every such line as a declared variable.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity (deployment values, set by the deploy wizard)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No variable is added here: the platform's own template above already declares
# every identity variable, and a second declaration would show twice in the
# deploy wizard. What this deployment sets for them (docs/specs/cloud-app.md,
# section 3.4):
#
# - TENANCY_MODE is single, the template default: one organization, every
#   person joins it as a viewer.
# - INITIAL_ADMIN_EMAIL is the lab owner's own Google address (the placeholder
#   ADMIN_EMAIL in the docs). It skips the allowlist and becomes system admin
#   plus organization admin. Never commit the real address.
# - GOOGLE_CALLBACK_URL is the cloud's public address followed by
#   /api/auth/google/callback, and the Google OAuth client lists exactly that
#   one redirect URI.
# - GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID and GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET come from the Google Cloud
#   console and are never committed.
# - Everyone else is added by address, one at a time, at
#   /admin/settings/allowlist (docs/runbooks/10-lab-operations.md). Never a
#   whole domain.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the production deployment sets for the platform's own variables
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These variables are declared once, above, by the platform's template, so they
# are not repeated here (docs/specs/cloud-app.md, section 3.13, and the runbook
# docs/runbooks/10-lab-operations.md, Part 9). The values of the production
# deployment at https://ai.marin.cr:
#
# - APP_URL is https://ai.marin.cr. The device-flow verification address that
#   nodes show is built from it.
# - JWT_SECRET and COOKIE_SECRET are generated by the deploy wizard.
# - SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY is generated by the deploy wizard and MUST be backed
#   up the day it is created (password manager and the ark drive's
#   inventory.local.yaml). It encrypts every node's llama key and every stored
#   credential: without it they cannot be read again, and a restored database
#   is useless without it. Never rotate it casually.
# - POSTGRES_HOST, POSTGRES_PORT, POSTGRES_DB, POSTGRES_USER, POSTGRES_PASSWORD
#   and POSTGRES_SSL point at the VPS Postgres on its private Docker network
#   (host infra-postgres), database local_ai, role local_ai_app, which is an
#   ordinary role (NOSUPERUSER, NOBYPASSRLS). Never the public port 5432.
# - JOBS_WORKER_MODE is all, and EVENT_BUS_ADAPTER is in-process (one replica;
#   postgres only if the API is ever scaled out).
# - OTEL_ENABLED is false. CORS_ORIGIN stays unset (same origin); the gateway
#   sets its own CORS headers.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gateway switches (docs/specs/gateway.md, section 3.7)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The OpenAI-compatible gateway reads these two before any settings page
# exists. The gateway items that use them are built after the cloud deploy;
# declaring them here keeps the first deployment's .env complete.

# Turns the /v1 gateway on (true) or off (false). Off answers 503 (LAI-GW-051).
LOCALAI_GATEWAY_ENABLED=true

# Which node address the gateway calls: public (the node's public https name)
# or tailnet (its tailnet address, when the VPS is on the tailnet).
LOCALAI_GATEWAY_UPSTREAM=public

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Catalog checker (docs/specs/cloud-app.md, section 4.6)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The API image carries the agent's static aiagent binary, and aictl is a
# symlink to it. The cloud runs `aictl catalog lint --json` on every catalog
# it imports or saves; without it a version stays a draft (doctor LAI-CLD-033).

# The agent release the production API image is built with: the part of the
# release tag after node-v (release tag node-v0.1.0 means 0.1.0, and
# node-v0.1.0-rc.1 means 0.1.0-rc.1). It must name a release that exists on
# GitHub with its aiagent-linux-amd64 and SHA256SUMS files, otherwise the image
# build stops with an error and no image is made. The image build downloads that
# release's aiagent and checks it against SHA256SUMS. It is needed only when the
# production image is built; the development image has no checker. Set it before
# the first deploy, and again whenever you move the cloud to a newer agent
# release.
AIAGENT_VERSION=

# Where the API finds the checker inside the image. Leave it as it is.
LOCALAI_AICTL_PATH=/opt/localai/bin/aictl
